# --- caddy:2-alpine (TTLI_Audit_Report_2026-09-02.md M3 — newly added to
# the scan loop). Checked Docker Hub directly (2026-09-02): 2-alpine is
# already an alias for 2.11.4-alpine, pushed ~1 month ago — this is the
# current official build, not a stale tag we picked; nothing to bump to.
# CVE-2026-27145/33818/39821/39822/42504/56853/56858/56859/56860/56862
# and CVE-2026-14456 are already covered by the postgres:18-alpine/
# openssl entries above (same stale-Go-toolchain/openssl findings,
# shared because both images bundle a binary built against the same
# base). c-ares and curl/libcurl are bundled OS packages this image's
# entrypoint never invokes (Caddy's own binary does its own HTTP/TLS/DNS
# in pure Go — no shell-out to curl, no cgo resolver) — same "waiting on
# upstream's next rebuild" situation as every OS-package entry above.
CVE-2026-33630 exp:2026-11-26
CVE-2026-5773 exp:2026-11-26
CVE-2026-6276 exp:2026-11-26
# The next three are vendored Go modules compiled directly into the
# caddy binary itself, not OS packages — worth a sharper look than the
# above precisely because caddy is the one component in this deployment
# with a host-published port (80/443, Caddyfile in
# infra/docker-compose.single-vm.yml). None is reachable through this
# deployment's actual Caddyfile (reverse_proxy + automatic HTTPS only):
# golang.org/x/crypto/ssh is a transitive dependency Caddy's default
# build never imports an SSH client/server from (no SSH-proxying module
# in this image); google.golang.org/grpc backs plugin-only features
# (e.g. a gRPC-based storage/cluster backend) this Caddyfile doesn't
# configure. golang.org/x/net/dns/dnsmessage and golang.org/x/text ARE
# on Caddy's real request/proxy path, but both CVEs need a malicious
# response Caddy itself requests (a DNS answer to Caddy's own upstream
# lookup, or text-processing of Caddy-originated data) — not something
# an inbound HTTP client controls directly.
CVE-2026-46600 exp:2026-11-26
CVE-2026-56852 exp:2026-11-26
CVE-2026-56854 exp:2026-11-26
CVE-2026-84304 exp:2026-11-26

# --- clamav/clamav-debian@sha256:4c975c43... two further Debian trixie
# findings, both `affected` with no patched package on any tier as of
# 2026-09-08, in an image this repo only pulls. Reviewed against the exact
# pinned image rather than the `stable` tag, which is mutable.
#
# CVE-2026-16742 is a systemd-homed local privilege escalation. Trivy
# attributes it to libsystemd0/libudev1 — the shared libraries — but
# systemd-homed is the vulnerable component, and it is not in this image:
# `/usr/lib/systemd/systemd-homed`, `/lib/systemd/systemd-homed` and
# `homectl` are all absent, and there is no homed-managed user for the bug
# to escalate through. Nothing but the libraries ships here.
CVE-2026-16742 exp:2026-12-07
# CVE-2026-86140 is a strcat stack-based buffer overflow in libxml2's
# `xmlSnprintfElements` (valid.c). This one is worth the sharpest look in
# the file, because clamd is the single component that parses
# attacker-controlled bytes by design — every learner upload reaches it
# (04 §3, REQ-BYPASS-08) — `libclamav.so.12` does link libxml2, and
# `ScanXMLDOCS` is on by default, so OOXML uploads really are parsed as
# XML. It is nevertheless unreachable: `xmlSnprintfElements` exists only
# to format DTD content-model validation errors, and libclamav never
# validates. Its dynamic symbol table references only the streaming
# xmlTextReader API — xmlReaderForMemory/ForFd/ForIO, xmlTextReaderRead,
# xmlTextReaderNext and friends — and contains no `xmlValidate*`, no
# `xmlParseDTD`, no `xmlSnprintfElements`, and no parser-property call
# that would turn validation on. xmlTextReader does not DTD-validate
# unless explicitly asked. Re-verify this symbol claim, not just the
# version, if libclamav is ever rebuilt against a newer libxml2.
CVE-2026-86140 exp:2026-12-07

# --- caddy:2-alpine@sha256:5f5c8640... five findings disclosed since the
# 2026-09-02 review above, checked 2026-09-08 against the same digest the
# single-VM compose pins. `2-alpine` is still an alias for 2.11.4-alpine
# (pushed 2026-06-24) and there is still no newer non-builder alpine tag,
# so there remains nothing to bump to; the four curl CVEs are `fixed` at
# 8.22.0-r0 in Alpine but this image ships 8.19.0-r0 and its maintainers
# have not rebuilt.
#
# The four curl/libcurl entries extend the existing curl/c-ares
# disposition verbatim: they are bundled OS packages Caddy's entrypoint
# never invokes. Caddy's binary does its own HTTP/TLS/DNS in pure Go —
# no shell-out to curl, no cgo resolver — so nothing in this container
# drives libcurl with attacker-influenced input.
CVE-2026-11352 exp:2026-12-07
CVE-2026-11586 exp:2026-12-07
CVE-2026-8925 exp:2026-12-07
CVE-2026-9547 exp:2026-12-07
# GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf is google.golang.org/grpc vendored into the caddy
# binary — the same plugin-only surface as CVE-2026-56852 above. This
# deployment's Caddyfile configures reverse_proxy and automatic HTTPS
# only; no gRPC-based storage or cluster backend is built in or
# configured, so the module is present but never reached.
GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf exp:2026-12-07

# CVE-2026-84445 is another gRPC-Go xDS-server crash disclosed after the
# review above. Re-checked 2026-09-09: caddy:2-alpine still resolves to the
# pinned 2.11.4 digest, so no official replacement exists. The affected code
# is an xDS server handling malformed resource names; this stock image and
# infra/Caddyfile build/configure only Caddy's HTTP reverse proxy and automatic
# HTTPS, with no xDS server or gRPC storage/cluster plugin. The CI workflow
# grants this exception to CADDY_IMAGE only, so the same CVE remains blocking
# anywhere else. Remove on the next official Caddy rebuild, or re-review at
# expiry.
CVE-2026-84445 exp:2026-12-08

# --- ttli-api: Debian 13 libexpat1 2.8.3-1~deb13u1. Trivy first reported
# these two advisories on 2026-09-09 and Debian publishes no fixed version.
# They require an application to parse crafted XML (hash-flood DoS and memory
# corruption respectively). TTLI's API source has no xml/ElementTree/expat/
# lxml/defusedxml import and no XML ingestion endpoint; uploaded SVG is rejected
# as an unsupported logo type rather than parsed. libexpat remains because it is
# a dependency of the CPython runtime, so it cannot safely be removed. The
# release workflow grants these entries to ttli-api:ci only; web, relay and all
# third-party images remain blocking. Remove as soon as Debian publishes a fix,
# or re-review source reachability at expiry.
CVE-2026-76956 exp:2026-12-08
CVE-2026-76957 exp:2026-12-08
